<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1989">1989</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as APEC</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation is an intergovernmental forum of 21 Pacific Rim economies founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canberra">Canberra</a> in 1989 at the initiative of the Australian prime minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bob-hawke">Bob Hawke</a>. It promotes free trade and economic integration in the region through annual leaders' meetings and agreed goals, and its members include the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/asia-pacific-economic-cooperation.jpg" alt="Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation" width="320" height="163" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cflm001 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Asia-Pacific_Economic_Cooperation_nations.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The inspiration for APEC came from the post-ministerial conferences of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/association-of-southeast-asian-nations">Association of Southeast Asian Nations</a>, launched in the mid-1980s, which showed the value of regular meetings between developed and developing economies. These led the Australian prime minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bob-hawke">Bob Hawke</a> to believe in the need for region-wide economic co-operation, and in January 1989 he called for more effective co-operation across the Pacific Rim. The first meeting took place in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canberra">Canberra</a> in November 1989, chaired by the Australian foreign affairs minister Gareth Evans and attended by ministers from twelve countries.</p><p>The organisation was at first an Australian initiative intended to secure greater Japanese engagement in the region. Its early years saw tension between members favouring a structured trade agreement and those, like the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, who preferred a looser, consultative approach.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first four annual meetings were attended by ministers. From 1993 they became Economic Leaders' Meetings, attended by heads of government from every member except <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a>, which sends a ministerial-level official. In 1994 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogor">Bogor</a> the leaders adopted the Bogor Goals, aiming at free and open trade and investment by 2010 for industrialised economies and by 2020 for developing ones. In 1995 an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka">Osaka</a> meeting agreed to set up the APEC Business Advisory Council from 1996.</p><p>At Vancouver in 1997, Royal Canadian Mounted Police officers used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a> spray on protesters who objected to the presence of leaders such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>'s president <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suharto">Suharto</a>. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> in 2001 the leaders endorsed the Shanghai Accord, committing members to reduce trade transaction costs by five percent over five years. APEC began discussing a Free Trade Area of the Asia-Pacific in 2006, an idea traced to a proposal by the Japanese economist Kiyoshi Kojima in 1966.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>APEC calls its members economies rather than countries, because the criterion for membership is being an independent economic entity rather than a sovereign state. Its 21 members include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a>, participating as Chinese Taipei, alongside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a>. It has three official observers: the ASEAN Secretariat, the Pacific Economic Cooperation Council and the Pacific Islands Forum Secretariat. The Secretariat is based in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a>, and the meeting rotates annually among members. In 1993 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> president Bill Clinton insisted on informal attire and gave the leaders leather bomber jackets, which began the tradition of leaders dressing in the host's national costume for the group photograph.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After 1945 Australia became an active middle power: the foreign minister H.V. Evatt was President of the United Nations General Assembly in 1948–1949, and Australia helped create the Colombo Plan of 1950 and the <b>Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation</b> forum in 1989.</blockquote><a href="/empire/commonwealth-of-australia">Commonwealth of Australia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>A founding member of ASEAN in 1967, Singapore hosts the secretariats of <b>APEC</b> and the Pacific Economic Cooperation Council, belongs to the Commonwealth, the Non-Aligned Movement and the East Asia Summit and keeps diplomatic relations with more than 180 states.</blockquote><a href="/empire/singapore">Singapore · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
